TCP/UDP socket is documented in RFC  2553.   Basically,  it  says
this:  A  specific  bind  on  an socket with an address specified
should accept IPv6 traffic to that address only.  If you  perform
a  wildcard  bind  on  an  socket to IPv6 address and there is no
wildcard bind socket on that TCP/UDP port, IPv6 traffic  as  well
as  IPv4  traffic  should be routed to that socket.  IPv4 traffic
should be seen as if it came from an IPv6 address  like  This  is
called an IPv4 mapped address.  If there are both a wildcard bind
socket  and  a  wildcard  bind  socket  on one TCP/UDP port, they
should behave separately.  IPv4 traffic should be routed  to  the
socket  and  IPv6  should  be routed to the socket.  However, RFC
2553 does not define the ordering constraint between calls to nor
how IPv4 TCP/UDP port numbers and IPv6 TCP/UDP port  numbers  re‐
late  to each other should they be integrated or separated Imple‐
mented behavior is very different from kernel to kernel.   There‐
fore, it is unwise to rely too much upon the behavior of wildcard
bind  sockets.   It  is recommended to listen to two sockets, one
for and another for when you would like to accept both  IPv4  and
IPv6 traffic.  It should also be noted that malicious parties can
